course: dictionary
lesson: 10724
title: One small word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Up» is one of the shortest words in English, but it does lots of jobs. It can show a direction, a place, an amount, or a state. The words around it are the clues that show which meaning is right. In «Breakfast takes 20 minutes, so he must get up by 7:00», getting up means leaving your bed, because you need to be awake for breakfast.
explain: Use the swap test. In «Prices went up», up means higher in amount, so the price got bigger. In «climb up the tree», up means toward a higher place. Guess the meaning from the clues, then check that your meaning fits the sentence.
explain: Up also joins with other words to make phrases that mean something new. «Giving up» means stopping, as in «a short break and asking for help work better than giving up». «Tie it up» means fasten it, as in «a painter is the rope at the front of a boat used to tie it up». Learn the phrase as one unit, not just the word up.
explain: Casual and formal talk use up in different ways. «What's up?» means «What is happening?» In formal writing you would say «The price increased by 2 dollars» instead of «they upped the price by 2». Choosing the right version helps your writing fit the reader.
fact: Up can show a direction, a place, an amount, or a state.
fact: «Up» means toward a higher place: climb up the tree.
fact: «Up» means out of bed: he must get up by 7:00.
fact: «Giving up» means stopping, and «tie it up» means fasten it with a rope.
fact: «What's up?» is a casual way to ask «What is happening?»
exercise: «Prices went up again this year.» What does up mean here? | Toward a high place, like a hill | *More or higher in amount | Finished, with nothing left | Out of bed
exercise: «Breakfast takes 20 minutes, so he must get up by 7:00.» What does get up mean? | Go to the shops | *Rise out of bed | Climb a ladder | Eat everything up
exercise: «When something is frustrating, a short break works better than giving up.» What does giving up mean? | Keep trying harder | Going up a hill | *Stop trying
test: Nadia says, «Eat it up», and means eat all of it. What does up mean here? | Toward a higher place | Out of bed | Along the street | *Completely, until nothing is left
test: «A painter is the rope at the front of a boat used to tie it up.» What does tie it up mean? | Paint it | Lift it high | *Fasten it | Wash it
exam: «You up for footy after school?» In this casual question, what does up for it mean? | *Willing to do it | Finished with it | Standing on a high place | Gone up in price
